{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And they are diminished</b> — Until now He has mentioned that He turns lands into barrenness and settles desolations. Now He will recount that He impoverishes and enriches. We find in the holy language that verbs are mentioned without their object being stated, except at the end — like אַרְאֶנּוּ וְלֹא עַתָּה אֲשׁוּרֶנּוּ וְלֹא קָרוֹב (Numbers 24:17), and who is this? This is a star that shall step out of Jacob, and further clarified: הִבִּיטוּ אֵלָיו וְנָהָרוּ (Psalms 34:6). And another verse explains the vav joined to it, namely: זֶה עָנִי קָרָא (Psalms 34:7). Likewise here: וַיִּמְעֲטוּ — and who are they? They are the princes mentioned afterward in the second verse. And behold, it is the opposite of וַיְבָרֶךְ אוֹתָם — וַיִּמְעֲטוּ." "<b>Through oppression</b> — womb, as in כִּי עָצָר עָצַר ה'. The opposite of וַיִּרְבֵּ. And וָרָעָה — the opposite of וַיְבָרֶךְ אוֹתָם. Now, רָעָה — diseases in the body, and יָגוֹן — in the heart. Also the vav of וְיָגוֹן draws distributively, like אֹדֶם פִּטְדָה וְבָרֶקֶת: מֵעֹצֶר וּמֵרָעָה וּמִיָּגוֹן."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 107, :notes {0 "The phrase 'the second verse' appears to refer to the second strophic unit of the psalm, not verse 2, since the referent 'the princes' is not transparently found there."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:39:13.766579075Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}